# Manuscript PDF page 228

                       HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   I asked if Mikey was still there.
   He lifted his hand into my view.
   “I’m here.”
   I asked again; the answer wouldn’t stay in my head.
   He stayed.
   I wanted the stones covered. Yanaisa showed me La Madre in her
cloth and Yúcahu held against her chest. Mikey tapped my shirt pocket
twice, where the coin was. No one had turned any of it into a trophy.
   I tried to raise my right hand; two fingers trembled and would not
settle.
   “Don’t keep testing it,” said Yari.
   “Is it—”
   “We’re getting you checked.” She didn’t borrow an answer to com‐
fort me.
   Street air came in through the loading door with the noise of arriv‐
ing crews. To my left: a radio, a wheel crossing a threshold, Manny ask‐
ing somebody to avoid stepping on the cracked drum. To my right:
nothing.
   While I lay there, the people we had brought kept working without
me. Sofía was spelling her last name for somebody. Vanessa was walk‐
ing someone through the latch again. Pri counted water bottles and
asked who still needed checking. The players had gone quiet. Some‐
where a person hummed a tune I knew, and it had no eight-second
hole.
   The clock over the stage said 10:18. For the first time since March I
hadn’t been counting.
   Mikey crouched beside me.
   “Doña Altagracia’s on the stairs,” he said. “Mom called her. They’re
both in the lobby arguing about who gets the front seat of whatever’s
taking you.”
   “Who’s winning?”
   “They’re both losing.” He wiped his face with his sleeve. “I told
them you’re an idiot. They said they know.”
   I tried to laugh; it hurt too much to finish.




